  <ArchiveNote slot="note" />
  <h2>Introduction</h2>
  <p>Artificial intelligence is moving beyond automation. It is becoming economically autonomous.</p>
  <p>As AI agents begin to transact within digital markets, blockchain networks, decentralized finance (DeFi) systems, and tokenized asset ecosystems, the structure of economic participation is shifting.</p>
  <p>The transition is not merely technological. It is architectural.</p>
  <h2>The shift from labor to agency</h2>
  <p>Traditional economic systems are built around wage-based labor. AI introduces a structural discontinuity. Autonomous agents can now execute smart contracts, manage digital asset portfolios, negotiate marketplace transactions, optimize decentralized infrastructure and operate continuously across blockchain networks.</p>
  <p>This redefines the boundary between human participation and machine execution.</p>
  <h2>Blockchain as settlement infrastructure</h2>
  <p>Agentic systems require trustless execution environments. Blockchain Layer-1 networks provide security and immutability. Layer-2 networks enable scalability and micro-transactions. Smart contracts automate execution logic.</p>
  <p>Without decentralized settlement layers, AI agents remain platform-dependent.</p>
  <h2>Self-sovereign identity as economic anchor</h2>
  <p>Digital sovereignty is inseparable from identity control. Self-Sovereign Identity frameworks ensure that AI agents operate under delegated authority rather than centralized platform ownership. In this architecture, identity becomes programmable, consent becomes auditable and data becomes licensable.</p>
  <p>SSI prevents agentic economies from collapsing into centralized extraction models.</p>
  <h2>Regulatory and governance implications</h2>
  <p>AI governance, cryptocurrency regulation, and digital asset policy frameworks are rapidly evolving. Questions emerging for policymakers include: should AI agents have legal standing? How should decentralized identity be regulated? What compliance structures apply to Layer-2 ecosystems? How should tokenized data markets be supervised?</p>
  <p>Agentic systems intersect directly with MiCA, DORA, digital sovereignty law, and AI governance frameworks globally.</p>
  <h2>Institutional strategy in the agentic era</h2>
  <p>Boards and regulators must evaluate infrastructure resilience, cryptographic security, post-quantum readiness, identity delegation frameworks and decentralized governance structures.</p>
  <p>Strategic positioning now requires literacy across AI systems, Web3 identity, and blockchain infrastructure.</p>
  <h2>Conclusion</h2>
  <p>The agentic economy is not a speculative concept. It is an infrastructural evolution.</p>
  <p>AI agents, self-sovereign identity, blockchain Layer-1 and Layer-2 networks, and privacy-preserving cryptography are converging into a new economic architecture.</p>
  <p>Institutions that understand this transition early will shape the regulatory, technological, and governance frameworks of the next decade.</p>
</Thesis>
